\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Eventually geometric coefficients converge}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Fix an integer #{p\ge 2}, a depth #{k\ge 0}, and real
numbers #{\lambda>0} and #{m>0}. Let #{(c_j)_{j\ge 0}} be
positive reals such that #{\frac{c_j}{\lambda^j}} tends to
#{1}, and for each integer #{K>k} let #{\tau_K>0} satisfy
that #{\frac{\tau_K}{m\lambda^K}} tends to #{1} as
#{K\to\infty}. Let #{\xi^{(K)}} have the centered Gaussian
law of the hierarchical precision on the residues modulo
#{p^K} with coefficients #{c_j} for #{j<K} and top
coefficient #{\tau_K}, as in \ref{fdip-009A}. Let #{S_K} be
the composition of the rescaled class means of the
\vocabk{fixed-ratio precision}{fdip-00BY} from depth #{K}
down to depth #{k}. As #{K\to\infty}, the law of
#{S_K\xi^{(K)}} converges to #{\nu_k}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{n=K-k} and abbreviate the one-depth map of
\ref{fdip-00C1} by #{T}. That proposition divides every
coefficient by #{\lambda} and moves it one index downward,
the old top coefficient included. After #{n} applications,
the coefficient in position #{i} on the residues modulo
#{p^k} is the original coefficient in position #{i+n},
divided by #{\lambda^n}. For #{i<k} one has #{i+n<K}, so the
coefficient is #{\frac{c_{n+i}}{\lambda^n}}. The top
position #{i=k} reads #{\tau_K}, and its image is
#{\frac{\tau_K}{\lambda^n}}.}
\p{The lower coefficient equals
#{\lambda^i\frac{c_{n+i}}{\lambda^{n+i}}}, which tends to
#{\lambda^i} because #{n+i\to\infty}. The top coefficient
equals #{m\lambda^k\frac{\tau_K}{m\lambda^K}}, which tends
to #{m\lambda^k}. The projectors on this fixed residue ring
do not depend on #{K}. By \ref{fdip-009A} each coefficient
is the eigenvalue on the image of the corresponding
projector. Every coefficient is positive for every #{K},
and the coefficients converge to the positive coefficients
of #{Q_k}. The inverse multiplies each image by the
reciprocal of its coefficient, and those reciprocals
converge. In the counting basis, the matrices of these
covariance operators therefore converge to the matrix of
#{Q_k^{-1}}.}
\p{Each pushforward is centered Gaussian. Its characteristic
function is #{\ue^{-\frac{1}{2}h(\zeta,C_K\zeta)}}, where
#{h} is the counting pairing and
#{C_K} is the covariance operator just obtained. In the
counting basis this quadratic form is the dot product of
#{\zeta} with the matrix of #{C_K}, by the same
eigenchart computation as in \ref{fdip-00C1}. Pointwise
convergence to the continuous characteristic function of
#{\nu_k} determines the limiting law. The covariances
themselves are the content of \ref{fdip-000B}.}
}
